"firstName":"M G","initials":"MG"},{"fullName":"Bell JI","lastName":"Bell","firstName":"J I","initials":"JI"},{"fullName":"McMichael AJ","lastName":"McMichael","firstName":"A J","initials":"AJ"},{"fullName":"Davis MM","lastName":"Davis","firstName":"M M","initials":"MM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6868-657X"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5127-4659"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6868-657X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","protocols","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5284","volume":"274","journalIssueId":967728,"dateOfPublication":"1996 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":1996,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Science","title":"Science (New York, N.Y.)","essn":"1095-9203","issn":"0036-8075","nlmid":"0404511","isoabbreviation":"Science"},"printPublicationDate":"1996-10-01"},"pubYear":"1996","pageInfo":"94-96","abstractText":"Identification and characterization of antigen-specific T lymphocytes during the course of an immune response is tedious and indirect. To address this problem, the peptide-major histocompatability complex (MHC) ligand for a given population of T cells was multimerized to make soluble peptide-MHC tetramers. Tetramers of human lymphocyte antigen A2 that were complexed with two different human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)-derived peptides or with a peptide derived from influenza A matrix protein bound to peptide-specific cytotoxic T cells in vitro and to T cells from the blood of HIV-infected individuals. In general, tetramer binding correlated well with cytotoxicity assays. This approach should be useful in the analysis of T cells specific for infectious agents, tumors, and autoantigens.","affiliation":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, CA 94305-5428, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S.","Researc